Now I see the whole function. Every append is `off += snprintf(buf + off, size - off, ...)` — **none of them clamp `off`**. This is the systemic bug. Even ignoring skills, any single truncation (e.g., a fat tool list or long memory file) poisons `off` and every subsequent append underflows `size - off`.

So the fix must be a **safe append helper** used throughout:

```c
/* Append and keep `off` inside the buffer. snprintf returns the length it
 * WOULD have written, so a single truncation would leave off > size and make
 * every later (size - off) underflow into a huge size_t — i.e. a stack smash.
 * Clamp instead: once full, stay full. */
static size_t append_fmt(char *buf, size_t size, size_t off,
    const char *fmt, ...)
{
    if (off >= size) {
        return size ? size - 1 : 0;
    }

    va_list ap;
    va_start(ap, fmt);
    int n = vsnprintf(buf + off, size - off, fmt, ap);
    va_end(ap);

    if (n < 0) {
        return off;
    }

    if ((size_t)n >= size - off) {
        return size - 1;   /* truncated: buffer is now full */
    }

    return off + (size_t)n;
}
```

Then replace all `off += snprintf(buf + off, size - off, ...)` with `off = append_fmt(buf, size, off, ...)`.

That's a clean, minimal, robust fix.

